# XCU 2.6-word-expansions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       The result of a parameter expansion is not expanded again (no
           further tilde, parameter, command or arithmetic expansion); only
           field splitting and filename expansion follow it.
test       t/001-no-reexpansion.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.6
class      required
says       Field splitting comes before filename expansion: the unquoted
           result of an expansion is split into fields and each field that
           holds pattern characters is then matched against file names.
test       t/002-split-then-glob.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.6
class      required
says       Filename expansion applies to unquoted fields with pattern
           characters (including those from expansions) and gives sorted
           matches, leaves a pattern that matches nothing as it was, is not
           done for quoted or escaped pattern characters, and is turned off
           by set -f.
test       t/003-pathname-switch.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.6
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.14.3
class      required
says       In filename expansion a star or question mark does not match a
           leading period, so a leading period needs an explicit period in
           the pattern, and a slash is matched only by a slash in the
           pattern.
test       t/004-pathname-rules.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6
class      required
says       A dollar sign that is not quoted and is followed by a space, tab
           or newline, or by nothing, stands for itself.
test       t/005-dollar-literal.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6
class      required
says       Expansions of a command's name and arguments are carried out in
           the current shell environment, so an assignment made by ${v:=w}
           or by $((n=5)) stays in effect, while one made inside a command
           substitution does not.
test       t/006-current-environment.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.1
class      required
says       An unquoted tilde at the start of a word, alone or up to the
           first slash, is replaced by the value of HOME; a tilde that is
           quoted or is not at the start of a word is not expanded.
test       t/007-tilde-home.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.1
class      required
says       In a variable assignment a tilde-prefix is also recognized after
           the equals sign and after each unquoted colon, and ends at the
           next colon or slash or at the end of the word.
test       t/008-tilde-assignment.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.1
class      required
says       The replacement for a tilde-prefix is treated as quoted, so it is
           not split into fields and not matched against file names, and an
           empty HOME gives one empty field.
test       t/009-tilde-result-quoted.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       Braces are optional around a name except where the next character
           could continue it, and an unbraced name is the longest valid
           name; for positional parameters of more than one digit braces are
           needed.
test       t/010-braces-and-names.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${p:-w} gives w when p is unset or null and ${p-w} only when p is
           unset; otherwise both give the value of p.
test       t/011-use-default.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${p:=w} assigns w to p when p is unset or null and ${p=w} only
           when p is unset; in all cases the final value of p is the result,
           and the assigned value has had quote removal.
test       t/012-assign-default.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.8.1
class      required
says       ${p:?w} gives an error when p is unset or null and ${p?w} only
           when p is unset: the shell writes the word to standard error and
           a non-interactive shell exits with a nonzero status before
           running the command; otherwise the value of p is the result.
test       t/013-error-if-unset.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.014
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${p:+w} gives w when p is set and not null, and ${p+w} gives w
           when p is set; in the other cases the result is null.
test       t/014-use-alternative.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.015
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       The word of a ${ } modifier is expanded only when its value is
           needed: with the parameter set and not null no side effect of the
           word takes place.
test       t/015-word-when-needed.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.016
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       The word of a ${ } modifier undergoes tilde expansion, parameter
           expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion and quote
           removal.
test       t/016-word-expansions.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.017
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${#p} is the decimal length in characters of the value of p, 0
           for a null value; ${#} is the number of positional parameters.
test       t/017-string-length.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.018
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.8.1
class      required
says       With set -u in effect the expansions ${#p} and the substring
           forms (${p%w}, ${p#w}) of an unset parameter fail: a
           non-interactive shell writes a diagnostic and exits with a
           nonzero status before the command runs.
test       t/018-nounset-fails.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.019
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${p%w} removes the smallest suffix matching the pattern w and
           ${p%%w} the largest; the pattern is a shell pattern, and a value
           with no matching suffix, or an empty pattern, leaves p unchanged.
test       t/019-remove-suffix.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.020
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       ${p#w} removes the smallest prefix matching the pattern w and
           ${p##w} the largest; a value with no matching prefix, or an empty
           pattern, leaves p unchanged.
test       t/020-remove-prefix.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.021
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       In the substring forms, pattern characters written unquoted, or
           arriving from an unquoted expansion, are special, also when the
           whole expansion is inside double quotes, and quoting a character
           inside the braces (or quoting the expansion that supplies the
           pattern) makes it literal.
test       t/021-pattern-quoting.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.022
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       The brace that ends a ${ } expansion is the first one not escaped
           by a backslash, not inside quotes and not inside an embedded
           expansion or command substitution.
test       t/022-closing-brace-search.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.023
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
says       A command substitution is replaced by the standard output of its
           commands in both forms, $( ) and backquotes, with every trailing
           newline removed and all other newlines kept.
test       t/023-cmdsub-basic.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.024
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
says       The commands of a command substitution run in a subshell
           environment: a variable they set or a directory they change to
           does not affect the current shell.
test       t/024-cmdsub-subshell.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.025
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
says       The result of a command substitution is not processed for further
           tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command substitution or
           arithmetic expansion.
test       t/025-cmdsub-not-reprocessed.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.026
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       The result of an unquoted command substitution is subject to
           field splitting (newlines split by default), and a quoted one
           gives a single field.
test       t/026-cmdsub-splitting.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.027
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
says       In the backquote form, a backslash keeps its literal meaning
           except before a dollar sign, backquote or backslash, where it is
           removed; inner backquotes are nested by escaping them with
           backslashes.
test       t/027-backquote-backslash.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.028
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
says       Command substitutions nest, and a substitution that starts with a
           subshell can be written with a space after the opening $(; its
           exit status is the status of the assignment it belongs to.
test       t/028-cmdsub-nesting.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.029
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.3
class      required
since      2024
says       For an expansion beginning with $(( the shell treats it as
           arithmetic only if it can be parsed that way; text that cannot be
           an arithmetic expression, such as two subshells in a row, is a
           command substitution.
test       t/029-cmdsub-or-arithmetic.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.030
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1
class      required
says       Arithmetic expansion evaluates the integer operators + - * / %
           and unary + and - with C precedence, parentheses, and C rules for
           division and remainder (the quotient is truncated toward zero and
           the remainder has the sign of the dividend).
test       t/030-arith-basic.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.031
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1
class      required
says       Arithmetic expansion has the C shift, relational, equality,
           bitwise and logical operators, which yield 1 or 0 for the
           relational, equality and logical ones.
test       t/031-arith-operators.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.032
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1
class      required
says       Arithmetic expansion has the conditional operator, takes decimal,
           octal (leading 0) and hexadecimal (0x) constants, reads variables
           by name, and expands parameters, command substitutions and blanks
           inside the expression.
test       t/032-arith-more.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.033
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1
class      required
says       The assignment operators = += -= *= /= %= <<= >>= &= ^= |= change
           the variable, the new value is the value of the expression, and
           the change is in effect after the expansion.
test       t/033-arith-assignment.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.034
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.1.2.1
class      required
says       As in C, the right side of && and || and the unchosen branch of
           the conditional operator are not evaluated.
test       t/034-arith-short-circuit.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.035
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
class      required
says       When a variable holds an integer constant, optionally with a
           leading plus or minus, $((x)) and $(($x)) give the same value.
test       t/035-arith-variable-forms.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.036
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.8.1
class      required
says       When the expression is invalid the expansion fails: the shell
           writes a diagnostic to standard error and a non-interactive shell
           exits with a nonzero status before running the command.
test       t/036-arith-invalid.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.037
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       With the default IFS a run of spaces, tabs and newlines in an
           unquoted expansion result separates fields, and leading or
           trailing white space gives no empty field.
test       t/037-split-default.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.038
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       Each non-white-space IFS character ends a field, so adjacent ones
           give empty fields, a leading one gives an empty first field, and
           a single trailing one adds no field; a lone one gives one empty
           field.
test       t/038-split-nonwhite.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.039
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       IFS white space next to a non-white-space IFS character is part
           of one delimiter, and leading and trailing IFS white space is
           ignored.
test       t/039-split-mixed.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.040
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       With IFS set to the empty string no field splitting happens, but
           an unquoted expansion that gives an empty field is removed
           (unless quoting is also present in that field).
test       t/040-split-null-ifs.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.041
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       Only the results of unquoted expansions are split: delimiter
           characters written literally in the word, or coming from a quoted
           expansion, do not split it.
test       t/041-split-only-results.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.042
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       An unquoted expansion that gives nothing or only IFS white space
           gives zero fields, not one empty field.
test       t/042-split-zero-fields.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.043
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       The results of unquoted command substitutions and arithmetic
           expansions are split at IFS characters like those of parameter
           expansions.
test       t/043-split-cmdsub-arith.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.044
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
says       A tab alone in IFS is a delimiter and space is then an ordinary
           character.
test       t/044-split-tab-only.sh

assertion  XCU.2.6-word-expansions.045
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.7
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2
class      required
says       Quote removal deletes the quoting backslashes, single quotes and
           double quotes of the word as written, but not a quote character
           that was itself quoted or that came out of an expansion.
test       t/045-quote-removal.sh
